issues.body:
**Environment:** Claude in Chrome 1.0.101, Brave (Chromium) on macOS, driven from Claude Code CLI.

### Problem

When a page in Claude's tab group calls `window.open(url, name, "width=…,height=…")`, the popup can't be used by any tool. It's missing from `tabs_context_mcp`, and targeting its tab id fails with:

> Tab N is not in Claude's tab group for this session. Tools can only target tabs inside the group.

Opening the popup URL in a regular tab is not a workaround, because the popup's content often doesn't come from its URL:

- The popup gets its content from the opener via `postMessage` (OAuth, pickers, payment, editors).
- Our admin app opens an empty named popup (`window.open('', 'edit_item', 'width=750,height=400,…')`), then sets the opener's form `target` to that name and submits the form (POST) into it. The popup only has a URL after the POST, and reloading that URL doesn't resend the form data. While the popup is open, it calls back into its opener through `window.opener`: it reads and sets fields in the opener's form and refreshes the opener on close.

In both cases the popup needs a live `window.opener` link to the original page, which a copy opened in a new tab doesn't have.

### Cause

Tool confinement, the tab list in `tabs_context_mcp` and `tabs_close_mcp` all require `tab.groupId === <Claude's group>`. A tab in a `popup` type window can never be in a tab group (Chromium only allows groups in `normal` windows), so a popup can never pass these checks. The extension also doesn't listen for new tabs (`tabs.onCreated` / `webNavigation.onCreatedNavigationTarget`), and `findOrphanedTabs()` in the bundle, which looks for ungrouped tabs by `openerTabId`, is never called.

### Proposed fix

If a tab has no group, use the group of its opener, following `openerTabId`:

```js
async function resolveGroupViaOpener(tab) {
	let current = tab;

	for (let depth = 0; depth < 5; depth++) {
		if (current.groupId !== chrome.tabGroups.TAB_GROUP_ID_NONE) {
			return current.groupId;
		}

		if (current.openerTabId === undefined || current.openerTabId === current.id) {
			break;
		}

		try {
			current = await chrome.tabs.get(current.openerTabId);
		} catch {
			break;
		}
	}

	return chrome.tabGroups.TAB_GROUP_ID_NONE;
}
```

Then:

1. Use `resolveGroupViaOpener(tab)` instead of `tab.groupId` in the tool confinement check and the `tabs_close_mcp` check.
2. In `tabs_context_mcp`, add tabs from `chrome.tabs.query({windowType: "popup"})` whose resolved group is the session's group.

I applied this to a local unpacked copy of 1.0.101 and tested it with the pages below. The popup showed up in `tabs_context_mcp`, and `read_page`, screenshot, click and typing all worked in it. The `postMessage` reply reached the opener, and `tabs_close_mcp` closed the popup. No other changes were needed, since screenshots and input already go through CDP per tab.

### Repro pages

`popup-test.html`:

```html
<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>Popup test</title>
<button id="open">Open popup</button>
<p>Popup says: <b id="reply">(nothing yet)</b></p>
<script>
let popup;

document.getElementById('open').onclick = function () {
	popup = window.open('popup-test-child.html', 'testPopup', 'width=420,height=320');
	popup.addEventListener('load', function () {
		popup.postMessage({ title: 'Configured by opener', button: 'Send back' }, '*');
	});
};

window.addEventListener('message', function (e) {
	if (e.source === popup) {
		document.getElementById('reply').textContent = e.data;
	}
});
</script>
```

`popup-test-child.html`:

```html
<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>Popup child</title>
<h2 id="title">Waiting for opener...</h2>
<input id="text" placeholder="Type here">
<button id="send" disabled>...</button>
<script>
window.addEventListener('message', function (e) {
	if (e.source !== window.opener) {
		return;
	}

	document.getElementById('title').textContent = e.data.title;
	const button = document.getElementById('send');
	button.textContent = e.data.button;
	button.disabled = false;
});

document.getElementById('send').onclick = function () {
	window.opener.postMessage(document.getElementById('text').value, '*');
};
</script>
```

Ask Claude to open `popup-test.html` (served from any local server), click "Open popup", type into the popup and press "Send back". Without the fix it can't see the popup. With it, the reply shows up on the opener page.

Related: #96516 (point 1).

### What version of Codex CLI is running?

Built from `main` at 8cae463a69

### What subscription do you have?

Any. Not plan-specific: the code path is shared by ChatGPT sign-in, API keys and custom providers.

### Which model were you using?

Any (the catalog refresh does not depend on the model)

### What platform is your computer?

Darwin 27.0.0 arm64

### What terminal emulator and version are you using (if applicable)?

Not terminal-specific (reproduced through `codex app-server`)

### Codex doctor report

```json
not available
```

### What issue are you seeing?

When `/models` cannot be reached (TLS-intercepting corporate proxy, firewall, offline) but the Responses endpoint can, every turn waits for the `/models` fetch to fail before the model is called. Each time, Codex logs:

```
ERROR codex_models_manager::manager: failed to refresh available models: request timed out
```

A failed fetch is never remembered, so nothing stops the next turn from trying again.

Root cause, at `main` 8cae463a69:

1. Every turn calls `list_models(RefreshStrategy::OnlineIfUncached, ...)`: [`turn_context.rs:929-934`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/8cae463a69aced7705440acaaa9c9cba2d36e056/codex-rs/core/src/session/turn_context.rs#L929-L934).
2. `OnlineIfUncached` fetches whenever `try_load_cache` misses: [`manager.rs:852-857`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/8cae463a69aced7705440acaaa9c9cba2d36e056/codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager.rs#L852-L857). After a failure there is no fresh cache entry, so it always misses.
3. `fetch_and_update_models` records a failure only in the access-rejection branch (401/403 or rejected credentials on a provider catalog, [`manager.rs:898-945`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/8cae463a69aced7705440acaaa9c9cba2d36e056/codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager.rs#L898-L945)). A network or timeout error returns at [`manager.rs:950`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/8cae463a69aced7705440acaaa9c9cba2d36e056/codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager.rs#L950) and leaves no trace.
4. On the ChatGPT backend, a `ModelsEtag` event in the response stream calls `refresh_if_new_etag` inline ([`turn.rs:3745-3749`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/8cae463a69aced7705440acaaa9c9cba2d36e056/codex-rs/core/src/session/turn.rs#L3745-L3749)). That path refreshes with `RefreshStrategy::Online` ([`manager.rs:777-803`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/8cae463a69aced7705440acaaa9c9cba2d36e056/codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager.rs#L777-L803)). After a failed fetch the in-memory catalog is the bundled fallback with no etag, so it never matches, and the same wait repeats mid-stream.

Each attempt can cost up to `MODELS_REFRESH_TIMEOUT` (5 s, [`models_endpoint.rs:49`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/8cae463a69aced7705440acaaa9c9cba2d36e056/codex-rs/model-provider/src/models_endpoint.rs#L49)). A successful fetch is reused for `DEFAULT_MODEL_CACHE_TTL` (300 s, [`manager.rs:43`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/8cae463a69aced7705440acaaa9c9cba2d36e056/codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager.rs#L43)); a failed one is reused for 0 s.

Related reports that show this symptom: #64059 (repeated `request timed out` refresh errors on every `exec`), #23119 (the same log line repeated through a run, with a different underlying error), #40137 (first-turn wait on `/models`).

### What steps can reproduce the bug?

Measured: a client process ran `codex app-server` behind an HTTPS proxy that the `/models` client could not use, while model requests still went through. Every uncached `model/list` call waited 2.86-3.19 s and returned the bundled catalog. Each turn's own `turn/start` also refetches through the same path (step 1 above), so every turn pays this wait again while `/models` stays unreachable.

Deterministic repro, as a unit test in `codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager_tests.rs`, using the existing `TestModelsEndpoint`: one `/models` timeout, then three turns, each doing an `OnlineIfUncached` refresh plus an etag refresh.

```rust
#[tokio::test(start_paused = true)]
async fn failed_fetch_is_remembered_for_cache_aware_refreshes() {
    // endpoint responses: [Err(RequestTimeout), Err(RequestTimeout), Ok(models)]
    for _ in 0..3 {
        manager.raw_model_catalog(RefreshStrategy::OnlineIfUncached, DEFAULT_HTTP_CLIENT_FACTORY).await;
        manager.refresh_if_new_etag("new-etag".into(), DEFAULT_HTTP_CLIENT_FACTORY).await;
    }
    assert_eq!(endpoint.fetch_count(), 1); // main: fails, every call fetched again
    ...
}
```

On `main` this fails at the first assertion: each of the six calls fetched again. With the patch below it passes, and `cargo test -p codex-models-manager` passes 61/61.

### What is the expected behavior?

A failed `/models` fetch should be remembered for the same window as a successful one. Until the window ends, cache-aware and etag refreshes should serve the current catalog (the last good snapshot or the bundled one) instead of paying the timeout on every turn. Explicit `RefreshStrategy::Online` refreshes and auth changes (a new endpoint identity) should still fetch at once.

### Additional information

Proposed patch (`models-manager` only; +33/-1 in `manager.rs`, one test, `tokio` `test-util` as a dev-dependency for paused time), based on `main` 8cae463a69:

- An in-memory `(endpoint identity, Instant)` records the last failed fetch that was not an access rejection. Access rejections keep their existing invalidation path.
- `OnlineIfUncached`/`Initialize` and `refresh_if_new_etag` skip the fetch while a failure for the current identity is younger than `DEFAULT_MODEL_CACHE_TTL`.
- A successful fetch clears the record. `RefreshStrategy::Online` always fetches.
- The test covers turns, etag refreshes, expiry after the TTL (paused tokio time) and the explicit online refresh.

Other agent CLIs handle this the same way. oh-my-pi's model manager waits 5 minutes before it retries a failed discovery under `online-if-uncached`, and its `online` strategy bypasses that wait. opencode refreshes models.dev on a 5-minute schedule, failures included, and never fetches per turn.

<details><summary>Patch</summary>

```diff
diff --git a/codex-rs/models-manager/Cargo.toml b/codex-rs/models-manager/Cargo.toml
index c4f4500c84..a865444be1 100644
--- a/codex-rs/models-manager/Cargo.toml
+++ b/codex-rs/models-manager/Cargo.toml
@@ -30,3 +30,4 @@ tracing = { workspace = true, features = ["log"] }
 pretty_assertions = { workspace = true }
 serde_json = { workspace = true }
 tempfile = { workspace = true }
+tokio = { workspace = true, features = ["test-util"] }
diff --git a/codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager.rs b/codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager.rs
index 551ce505af..f5855742ff 100644
--- a/codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager.rs
+++ b/codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager.rs
@@ -434,6 +434,8 @@ pub struct OpenAiModelsManager {
     endpoint_client: SharedModelsEndpointClient,
     catalog_source: CatalogSource,
     auth_manager: Option<Arc<AuthManager>>,
+    /// Identity and time of the last failed `/models` fetch that did not reject access.
+    failed_fetch: std::sync::Mutex<Option<(Option<String>, tokio::time::Instant)>>,
 }

 /// Static model manager backed by an authoritative in-process catalog.
@@ -509,6 +511,7 @@ impl OpenAiModelsManager {
             endpoint_client,
             catalog_source: CatalogSource::Default,
             auth_manager,
+            failed_fetch: std::sync::Mutex::new(None),
         }
     }

@@ -791,6 +794,11 @@ impl OpenAiModelsManager {
             return;
         }
         drop(refresh_guard);
+        // A new etag cannot be fetched through an endpoint that just failed; refetching here
+        // would move the wait into the response stream.
+        if self.fetch_recently_failed() {
+            return;
+        }
         if let Err(err) = self
             .refresh_available_models(RefreshStrategy::Online, &http_client_factory)
             .await
@@ -850,7 +858,7 @@ impl OpenAiModelsManager {
             }
             CatalogLoadMode::Initialize
             | CatalogLoadMode::Refresh(RefreshStrategy::OnlineIfUncached) => {
-                if self.try_load_cache(mode).await {
+                if self.try_load_cache(mode).await || self.fetch_recently_failed() {
                     return Ok(());
                 }
                 self.fetch_and_update_models(http_client_factory, mode)
@@ -941,6 +949,9 @@ impl OpenAiModelsManager {
                             error!("failed to invalidate models cache: {err}");
                         }
                     }
+                } else if !(access_denied || credentials_rejected) {
+                    *self.failed_fetch_slot() =
+                        Some((request_identity, tokio::time::Instant::now()));
                 }
                 if implicit_gateway && !credentials_rejected {
                     // Automatic discovery is optional. Let the picker use the available catalog.
@@ -950,6 +961,7 @@ impl OpenAiModelsManager {
                 return Err(error);
             }
         };
+        *self.failed_fetch_slot() = None;
         if Some(&identity) != self.endpoint_client.identity().as_ref()
             || (mode == CatalogLoadMode::Initialize
                 && self.catalog_is_initialized(&*self.remote_models.read().await))
@@ -979,6 +991,26 @@ impl OpenAiModelsManager {
         Ok(())
     }

+    fn failed_fetch_slot(
+        &self,
+    ) -> std::sync::MutexGuard<'_, Option<(Option<String>, tokio::time::Instant)>> {
+        self.failed_fetch
+            .lock()
+            .unwrap_or_else(std::sync::PoisonError::into_inner)
+    }
+
+    /// A cache-aware refresh treats a recent failed fetch for this identity like a fresh
+    /// entry: an unreachable `/models` would otherwise cost its timeout on every turn.
+    /// Explicit online refreshes and auth changes still fetch.
+    fn fetch_recently_failed(&self) -> bool {
+        let identity = self.endpoint_client.identity();
+        self.failed_fetch_slot()
+            .as_ref()
+            .is_some_and(|(failed_identity, failed_at)| {
+                *failed_identity == identity && failed_at.elapsed() < DEFAULT_MODEL_CACHE_TTL
+            })
+    }
+
     fn supports_api_key_discovery(&self) -> bool {
         self.endpoint_client.supports_api_key_models()
             && !self.endpoint_client.has_command_auth()
diff --git a/codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager_tests.rs b/codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager_tests.rs
index 6b0b895334..b6ef43c5f1 100644
--- a/codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager_tests.rs
+++ b/codex-rs/models-manager/src/manager_tests.rs
@@ -1386,6 +1386,58 @@ async fn failed_discovery_does_not_reuse_a_catalog_from_another_identity() {
     assert_eq!(manager.get_remote_models().await, Vec::<ModelInfo>::new());
 }

+#[tokio::test(start_paused = true)]
+async fn failed_fetch_is_remembered_for_cache_aware_refreshes() {
+    let models = vec![remote_model("remote", "Remote", /*priority*/ 0)];
+    let endpoint = Arc::new(TestModelsEndpoint {
+        uses_codex_backend: true,
+        responses: Mutex::new(
+            vec![
+                Err(CodexErr::RequestTimeout),
+                Err(CodexErr::RequestTimeout),
+                Ok(models.clone()),
+            ]
+            .into(),
+        ),
+        ..TestModelsEndpoint::default()
+    });
+    let manager = OpenAiModelsManager::new_with_cache(
+        Arc::new(TestModelsCache::default()),
+        endpoint.clone(),
+        Some(AuthManager::from_auth_for_testing(
+            CodexAuth::create_dummy_chatgpt_auth_for_testing(),
+        )),
+    );
+    let refresh_if_uncached = || {
+        manager.raw_model_catalog(
+            RefreshStrategy::OnlineIfUncached,
+            DEFAULT_HTTP_CLIENT_FACTORY,
+        )
+    };
+
+    // Each turn refreshes with OnlineIfUncached, and each response may carry a models etag;
+    // an unreachable `/models` must not cost its timeout on every one of them.
+    for _ in 0..3 {
+        refresh_if_uncached().await;
+        manager
+            .refresh_if_new_etag("new-etag".into(), DEFAULT_HTTP_CLIENT_FACTORY)
+            .await;
+    }
+    assert_eq!(endpoint.fetch_count(), 1);
+
+    // The failure is remembered only as long as a success would be.
+    tokio::time::advance(DEFAULT_MODEL_CACHE_TTL).await;
+    refresh_if_uncached().await;
+    assert_eq!(endpoint.fetch_count(), 2);
+
+    // An explicit online refresh still fetches.
+    manager
+        .raw_model_catalog(RefreshStrategy::Online, DEFAULT_HTTP_CLIENT_FACTORY)
+        .await;
+    assert_eq!(endpoint.fetch_count(), 3);
+    assert_models_contain(&manager.get_remote_models().await, &models);
+}
+
