## Summary

An extension can return a `compaction` draft from a boundary handler (`agent_before_settle` / `turn_end`). This is the typed route for compaction during a run (`firstKeptEntryId: string | null`). Pi persists it as a normal compaction entry, but it emits none of the events the other compaction paths emit. Extensions that react to compaction through `session_compact` never learn that the context was compacted. For example, a memory extension that archives the summary and injects a recovery notice misses it.

## Versions

- `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` 1.6.1
- macOS

## Where

In `dist/core/agent-session.js` (1.6.1):

- `_applyBoundaryDrafts` (~L637-656): a `compaction` draft goes to `manager.appendCompaction(summary, firstKeptEntryId, tokensBefore, details, true, usage)` (~L652-653). No event is emitted afterwards.
- `compact()` emits `session_compact` (~L2410) and `compaction_end` (~L2428/~L2442) after appending its entry.
- Auto-compaction emits `session_compact` (~L2699) and `compaction_end` (~L2714).

So the same kind of entry (`type: "compaction"`) is announced on two paths and silent on the third.

## Why the boundary path matters

`ctx.compact()` is fire-and-forget. In print mode (`pi -p`) the runtime is disposed as soon as `prompt()` resolves, and `dispose()` aborts a compaction that is still running ("Compaction cancelled"). Any follow-up message sent from `onComplete` never runs. A boundary draft is awaited inside the prompt, so it is the only route that works in every mode. Extensions that use it must currently choose: either work in print/JSON mode, or let `session_compact` listeners see the compaction.

## Reproduction (verified on 1.6.1)

`a.ts` returns a compaction draft from the settle boundary:
```ts
export default function (pi: any) {
	let done = false;
	pi.on("agent_before_settle", (event: any) => {
		if (done || event.outcome !== "completed") return;
		done = true;
		return { entries: [...event.entries, { type: "compaction", summary: "S", firstKeptEntryId: null }] };
	});
}
```
`b.ts` logs the compaction events it observes:
```ts
import { appendFileSync } from "node:fs";
const log = (line: string) => appendFileSync(process.env.EVENTS_LOG!, line + "\n");
export default function (pi: any) {
	pi.on("session_before_compact", (e: any) => void log(`session_before_compact reason=${e.reason}`));
	pi.on("session_compact", (e: any) => void log(`session_compact reason=${e.reason}`));
}
```
`c.ts` (control) compacts through `ctx.compact`, with a summary built in the hook so no LLM call is needed:
```ts
export default function (pi: any) {
	let done = false;
	pi.on("session_before_compact", () => ({ compaction: { summary: "S", firstKeptEntryId: null, tokensBefore: 0 } }));
	pi.on("agent_settled", (_e: any, ctx: any) => {
		if (done) return;
		done = true;
		ctx.compact({ onComplete: () => {}, onError: (err: Error) => console.error(err.message) });
	});
}
```
Runs:
```sh
EVENTS_LOG=$PWD/events-boundary.log pi --no-extensions -e a.ts -e b.ts -p --session-dir sessions-boundary 'Reply with the single word hi.'
# control: the session must exceed keepRecentTokens, so four 3000-line files are read first
EVENTS_LOG=$PWD/events-control.log pi --no-extensions -e c.ts -e b.ts -p --session-dir sessions-control \
  'Run `cat big1.txt`, `cat big2.txt`, `cat big3.txt`, `cat big4.txt`, each in its own tool call. Then reply with the single word hi.'
```

Results:

| Run | Session entries end with | Events logged by `b.ts` |
|---|---|---|
| boundary draft (`a.ts`) | `compaction` | none |
| control (`c.ts`, `ctx.compact`) | `compaction` | `session_before_compact reason=manual`, `session_compact reason=manual` |

The same kind of entry is persisted in both runs, but only the control run announces it.

## Expected

After persisting a boundary `compaction` draft, emit `session_compact` with the same payload shape as the other paths: the persisted compaction entry and a reason, for example `"extension"` or the boundary name. Emitting `compaction_start`/`compaction_end` for consistency would help UIs too. If emitting it from the boundary commit is undesirable, a documented alternative event would also work.

## Context

Found while building pi-compactor (a phased compaction extension: preflight handoff, safe points, pins). Today it uses `ctx.compact` in tui/rpc mode, so `session_compact` listeners work there, and boundary drafts in print/json mode, where they don't. With the event emitted for boundary drafts, it could use one route everywhere.

## Summary

On Discord, a reply with a Markdown table that crosses the 2,000-char message limit arrives partly as raw `| … |` text, whatever `markdown.tables` is set to. The outbound path splits the **raw** markdown at 2,000 chars first and converts tables **per piece** afterwards. The piece that holds the header row becomes a rendered table. The piece that starts mid-table has no header, so it passes through as raw pipes, sometimes starting mid-row.

## Versions

- `openclaw` 2026.10.4 (383c124)
- `@openclaw/discord` 2026.10.4
- `channels.discord.markdown.tables`: `block` (falls back to `code`) and `bullets` both reproduce it

## Where it happens (published dist, 2026.10.4)

1. `discordOutbound.chunker` (`extensions/discord/src/outbound-adapter.ts`) calls `chunkDiscordTextWithMode(text, { maxChars: limit, maxLines: ctx?.formatting?.maxLinesPerMessage ?? DISCORD_PRECHUNK_MAX_LINES })` with `DISCORD_PRECHUNK_MAX_LINES = Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`, on the raw text.
2. Each piece then goes to `sendMessageDiscord`, which calls `prepareDiscordOutboundText` → `renderDiscordMarkdown` → `convertMarkdownTables(markdown, tableMode)` (`extensions/discord/src/outbound-text.ts`), per piece.
3. `sendMessageDiscord` chunks the rendered piece again at `maxLinesPerMessage`.

The Discord chunker balances code fences across chunks, but it does not treat a pipe table as a unit. So step 1 can cut a table between rows, or inside a row, before step 2 ever sees it whole.

## Repro (synthetic text, no network)

```js
// node upstream-repro.mjs <path to @openclaw/discord/dist/.setup/send.receipt-*.mjs>
import { convertMarkdownTables } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/text-chunking";
const { b: chunkDiscordTextWithMode } = await import(process.argv[2]); // minified export in 2026.10.4

const intro = "Weekly summary.\n\n" + "Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. ".repeat(28) + "\n\n";
const rows = Array.from({ length: 12 }, (_, i) =>
  `| Day ${String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")} | Session ${i + 1} | ${(5 + i / 10).toFixed(1)} km | ${140 + i} bpm |`);
const reply = intro + "| Day | Session | Distance | HR |\n|---|---|---|---|\n" + rows.join("\n") + "\n\nEnd.";

const rawPipeLines = (c) => { let inFence = false, n = 0;
  for (const l of c.split("\n")) { if (/^\s{0,3}```/.test(l)) { inFence = !inFence; continue; }
    if (!inFence && /^\s*\|.*\|\s*$/.test(l)) n++; } return n; };
const count = (chunks) => chunks.reduce((a, c) => a + rawPipeLines(c), 0);

for (const mode of ["code", "bullets"]) {
  const today = chunkDiscordTextWithMode(reply, { maxChars: 2000, maxLines: Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER })
    .flatMap((p) => chunkDiscordTextWithMode(convertMarkdownTables(p, mode), { maxChars: 2000, maxLines: 17 }));
  const expected = chunkDiscordTextWithMode(convertMarkdownTables(reply, mode), { maxChars: 2000, maxLines: 17 });
  console.log(`tables=${mode}: today ${today.length} msgs, ${count(today)} raw pipe lines | convert-first ${expected.length} msgs, ${count(expected)} raw pipe lines`);
}
```

Output on 2026.10.4:

```
tables=code: today 3 msgs, 4 raw pipe lines | convert-first 2 msgs, 0 raw pipe lines
tables=bullets: today 4 msgs, 4 raw pipe lines | convert-first 4 msgs, 0 raw pipe lines
```

The reply is 2,179 chars and the table starts at char 1,615. On a real host, a 6,918-char agent reply was delivered as 12 Discord messages. A local replay of this pipeline reproduced all 12 message sizes to within one char, including the raw-pipe tail.

## Expected

Tables are converted before the 2,000-char pre-split, or the pre-split treats a pipe table as one unit and splits only between rows (the way `EmbeddedBlockChunker` already does for block streaming: "a Markdown table that fits in `maxChars` is kept in one chunk … larger tables split at row boundaries"). Either way, every piece that holds table rows should get the same rendering.

## Actual

The piece that starts mid-table keeps raw `| … |` pipes, and its first line can be the tail of a row cut mid-cell.

## Workaround

None by config. `maxLinesPerMessage` changes only the message count, and every `markdown.tables` mode shows the same raw tail.

### Problem

The Dream Diary (`DREAMS.md`) output is always in English because the diary-generation prompt is hardcoded inside the `memory-core` plugin. Users whose workspace language is non-English (e.g., Chinese) end up with a mixed-language workspace — all other files are in Chinese but Dream Diary entries are in English.

This is not just a cosmetic issue: it breaks workspace language consistency and requires manual post-editing after every dreaming sweep.

### Proposal

Add one of these config options under `plugins.entries.memory-core.config.dreaming`:

1. **`dreaming.language`** — simple locale/language hint (e.g., `"zh-CN"`, `"ja"`) that the diary prompt uses to steer output language.
2. **`dreaming.diaryPrompt`** — full custom prompt override for maximum flexibility (language, style, format, length).

Option 1 is the common case; option 2 is the power-user escape hatch. Both could coexist — if `diaryPrompt` is set, it takes precedence over `language`.

### Example

```jsonc
{
  "plugins": {
    "entries": {
      "memory-core": {
        "config": {
          "dreaming": {
            "enabled": true,
            "language": "zh-CN"
            // or:
            // "diaryPrompt": "用中文写一段简短的梦境日记，风格简洁，不超过三段。"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

### Environment

- OpenClaw version: latest
- OS: WSL2 (Linux)

prs.body:
Related to #192988. Thanks to @GoatInAHat for the original fix and regressions.

[Review the 17-file change against the main revision used for this repair](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/compare/107c145f25d0edee99b830f7ef38486641d769de...7a5b042ab52eb704ac5d3c04c56432c596aa5e15).

## What Problem This Solves

A chat goal can stay active with nothing running. A late native child result takes priority over the next goal turn. The parent replies to the result, then stops. This also happens when a reused worker reports an error that the parent handles successfully.

## User Impact

A successful parent can continue the same goal without another human message. Pause, Stop and queued human input take precedence. No configuration or migration is needed.

## Why This Change Was Made

For registered child returns, the selected harness or CLI receives the goal context. Detached native replies take a different path. The existing follow-up queue now keeps the original caller’s displaced turn until each competing native reply has a successful, confirmed delivery. It checks the session, goal and placement again. A child cannot grant permission to pursue a goal. No new scheduler, Codex plugin API or durable state was added.

## Evidence

### Checks for `7a5b042a`

Native returns now use the session store’s existing exact transcript check. The goal execution and settlement paths share their live owner checks. The regression changes the session ID and lifecycle revision separately and requires each change to suppress the native continuation. Ordinary continuations still follow the chat.

The session identity check on a local merge with `6f255f9f` had found 29 comparisons against 20 allowed in one file and 6 against 2 in the other. It now passes on the branch and the merged tree with the allowlist unchanged. On that main revision, six regressions failed without the repair. With the repair, 186 tests passed across nine suites, including four session-store suites. A host interruption split the run. After simplifying the test setup, I reran all 89 cases in the affected suite in 116.94 seconds including startup. Formatting and focused type-aware lint with type checking passed on all changed TypeScript files.

I exercised the same production change on a development Gateway based on `5e245aa0` with other recovery changes, a real Codex CLI 0.164.0 and scripted replies. In both completion cases the same worker returned after foreground settlement. The parent started one more automatic turn on the original goal. These elapsed times in milliseconds come from separate runs.

```text
success: parent reply 36841, next turn 37947, complete 38955 (turn 4)
handled child error: parent reply 38153, next turn 39126, complete 39744 (turn 4)
Stop: no more model calls or run starts for 8525 ms, goal paused (turn 3)
Pause: no more model calls or run starts for 8811 ms, goal paused (turn 3)
```

### Earlier controls at `a4e8d899`

- **Real Gateway and Codex, synthetic inference.** A single goal spawned a worker, waited for its first answer, reused it, and held its second answer until the foreground turn ended. With stock `82b682c6`, Codex CLI 0.164.0 and 0.169.0 both delivered the parent reply and stayed idle for 25 seconds. With the repair ported to that revision and CLI 0.169.0, the same goal advanced from turn 3 to 4 and completed. The handled child failure also completed once.
- **Negative controls.** Pause and Stop during the reply each prevented further provider calls. A restricted role could not create a sibling session or start inference on an external Codex connection that could not enforce the role. Neither refusal reached the provider.
- **Earlier source check at `a4e8d899`.** The new direct-handoff test failed on `107c145f` with no continuation queued. The candidate passed 141 focused tests, including mixed siblings, replay and stale authority, plus type-aware lint and formatting. Five single-worker test shards took 331 seconds including compilation. The goal and authority shards took 51 and 78 seconds, respectively.
- **Limits.** The local registry lacked the current locked `@openclaw/fs-safe`. The focused tests used the older checkout’s dependencies. Full local typechecking did not pass with that combination. Hosted checks must verify the exact lockfile. The CLI and revocation cases use fixtures. No live provider or production deployment was used.

### Output from the repaired Gateway

This is a field extract from the isolated Linux run of the `a4e8d899` repair backported to OpenClaw `82b682c6`, with Node 24.22.1, the real Codex 0.169.0 app server, and a loopback Responses fixture. It is not a build of the PR head’s dependency set. The fixture held the second answer until after the foreground reply. It sent one user request. Each sample checked the original goal ID.

Elapsed times are milliseconds. Identifiers are replaced consistently. The child’s second answer contained a unique marker. `received` records a terminal parent reply in Gateway history confirmed by `agent.wait`. The next automatic request included that marker. The two history rows come from the final read.

```text
session {"sessionId":"S","goalId":"G"}
operatorSend {"ms":17425,"runId":"U"}
send_input {"ms":24389,"childId":"C","submission":"T2"}
foreground {"ms":26195,"runId":"F","status":"ok"}
release {"reason":"foreground-settled","ms":31292}
child {"ms":31741,"nativeId":"C","parentNativeId":"P","turns":[{"turnId":"T1","terminal":"task_complete","answerRecorded":true},{"turnId":"T2","terminal":"task_complete","answerRecorded":true}]}
request {"ms":33595,"threadId":"P","auto":false,"freshMarker":true}
received {"ms":33978,"run":"announce:codex-native:P:C:succeeded","count":3}
lifecycle {"ms":34814,"runId":"N","phase":"start"}
request {"ms":34908,"threadId":"P","auto":true,"hasMarker":true}
chat.history {"run":"announce:codex-native:P:C:succeeded","terminal":true,"stop":"stop","origin":"codex-app-server"}
chat.history {"run":"N","terminal":true,"stop":"stop","origin":"codex-app-server"}
