```

### CLI control

A separate trial on the same build used an ordinary Gateway goal and a local JSON CLI fixture. Each run started two distinct subprocesses. A successful second reply advanced the goal until I paused it. Exit 1 on the second call paused the goal automatically.

```text
{"secondExit":0,"auto":[false,true],"terminal":["end","end"],"savedReplies":2,"continuations":1,"pausedBy":"operator"}
{"secondExit":1,"auto":[false,true],"terminal":["end","error"],"savedReplies":1,"continuations":0,"pausedBy":"error"}
```

Three independent reviewers checked the production changes. A separate review checked the shorter test fixture. The standalone automated reviewer could not start on this host. Please review the caller boundary and the behavior of simultaneous returns.

Related: #202672

## What Problem This Solves

When an Anthropic Messages stream ends with malformed tool-call arguments, OpenClaw rejects the call before execution but can still return a generic terminal failure. A configured fallback may be available, or the model may be able to repair the call, yet neither path currently handles this parser-proven rejection reliably.

## Why This Change Was Made

The exact transport error code proves the rejected call never reached a tool. OpenClaw now skips same-model blind retries, uses one bounded corrective continuation when no fallback is configured, and routes a silent replay-safe failure to a configured model fallback; active or side-effecting attempts remain excluded.

## User Impact

Malformed tool calls can recover through one valid continuation or a configured fallback model. If recovery is exhausted, the error tells users to check completed work, retry, or choose another model; bounded diagnostics remain secondary.

## Evidence

- Real Gateway proof used the HTTP Responses API and CLI with the production Anthropic Messages transport against localhost SSE. Base `9e7555d047e4246d9cbd3b3c94ccd2d1a5dce348` returned generic failures; head `8bcab00993c6f2b81b431ea198d9493268f1832b` recovered both entrypoints and returned the local read result to the model.
- On the head, each entrypoint saw one malformed response, issued one repaired tool call, executed one local read, and sent its result back to the model. Gateway logs retained `argumentChars=19`, `argumentHash=jiqoxq1e07seo`, and `repairAttempted=true`; raw `SECRET.md` argument text was absent from logs and responses.
- Existing recovery policy bounds the continuation to one attempt and uses current-attempt replay-safety evidence; focused regression covers the no-fallback continuation, configured model fallback, and a side-effecting failure that must not fall back.
- Exhausted failures now lead with the next user action and keep diagnostic details secondary; focused coverage checks the same copy in live error rendering and persisted history rendering.
- The MiniMax guide documents the model-level `supportsEagerToolInputStreaming: false` request-format opt-out for Anthropic Messages. The linked report's intermittent trigger remains unreproduced, so this setting is not presented as a confirmed fix.
- Red-on-base check ran the PR's `assistant-failure.test.ts` against base production code (using the literal error code because the named constant is introduced by this PR): the immediate-fallback and no-fallback recovery regressions failed, with no production files overlaid.

```text
$ node proof-live.mjs --expect-absent
