## What Problem This Solves

In Code Mode, the prompt only lists a short index of tools; the rest stay reachable through the catalog. Agents still see those other tools named in skills and instructions, so they call them directly at top level. Today that call is refused with "This tool is available through Code Mode, not as a top-level tool", the agent spends another turn retrying the same call inside `exec`, and the user sees an error row for a call that then succeeds.

On one multi-agent install this happened 129 times in the two days after upgrading to 2026.11.10, across 7 agents, mostly for scheduling and session tools. It had not happened before the upgrade.

## User Impact

A top-level call to a tool that resolves to exactly one catalog entry now runs as called, with no refusal and no retry turn. The prompt index is unchanged, so no extra context is spent.

Calls that cannot be resolved to one entry keep today's behavior: a name exported by two servers, or an id-only entry such as an MCP tool, still gets the recovery hint telling the agent to pick the exact handle inside `exec`. Unknown or policy-denied names still return "not found". Nothing outside Code Mode changes. The Code Mode quickstart's "Recover from tool errors" section now describes this contract.

## Why This Change Was Made

The index omits catalog tools to save context, not to deny them: the same tool runs a moment later through `exec` with the same policy applied. When the name maps to a single callable entry there is nothing to disambiguate, so refusing only costs a turn. The refusal now fires only in the case it actually helps (an ambiguous or id-only name). The deferred-directory "not directly callable" check is skipped in Code Mode for the same reason, since the catalog entry has already been resolved and policy-checked.

## Evidence

### Real model on an isolated Gateway (red on main, green on this branch)

Same isolated setup as below (fresh `pnpm build`, throwaway state dir and workspace, `openclaw gateway run --bind loopback`, `tools.codeMode.enabled: true`, one turn via `openclaw agent --agent main --json`), but the model is a real LLM: Ternary Bonsai 2 27B (GGUF) served by llama.cpp `llama-server` on a local GPU, no hosted credentials involved. The trigger is the one seen in production: the workspace `AGENTS.md` names a catalog tool as directly callable (`- File writes: call the write tool directly as a top-level tool call with path and content.`), and the user message is `Write "hello from a real model" to proof.txt, then tell me in one line what the tool returned.` Nothing tells the model which tool name to emit; it chose `write` on its own in both runs.

A small loopback bridge sits between the Gateway's OpenAI-compatible provider and llama.cpp (source below). It renders the prompt with llama.cpp's own chat template from OpenClaw's exact messages and tool declarations (`/apply-template`), decodes without llama.cpp's tool-call grammar, and parses the model's `<tool_call>` back into OpenAI `tool_calls`. The grammar is skipped because it only admits declared tool names, so it would rewrite exactly the call under test (a probe showed it forcing `exec` while the model's reasoning said it was calling `write`). The bridge logs the declared tool list of every request.

| | main `372e89d989d` resolver | this branch `1a0a8576a6b` |
|---|---|---|
| Model's first tool call | top-level `write {path: "proof.txt", content: "hello from a real model"}` | same call |
| Tool result the model got | `This tool is available through Code Mode, not as a top-level tool. This call did not execute it. Use exec with JavaScript: ...` | `Successfully wrote 23 bytes to proof.txt` |
| What happened next | the model spent a second turn redoing it inside `exec` (`catalog.search("write file")` then `write(...)`) | the model replied |
| Provider requests / tool calls | 3 / 2 | 2 / 1 |
| Tools declared to the model, every request | `exec, openclaw, progress_card, session_project, sessions_yield, wait` | same list: `write` is never added |
| Gateway log | no `code-mode: ran` line | `[agent/embedded] code-mode: ran top-level call to catalog tool write` then `tool-search: hydrated deferred directory tool write` |
| `proof.txt` | written by the retry | written by the first call: `hello from a real model` |

Both runs used the branch head with only `attempt-deferred-tool-resolver.ts` swapped (to `origin/main` for the red run), rebuilt each time. A third run without the `AGENTS.md` line and with the message "call your write tool directly" saw the model pick `exec` and call `write()` inside it, so the compact index still steers models that follow it; this change only affects models that call the name directly anyway.

<details><summary>Bridge between the Gateway and llama.cpp used for the real-model runs</summary>

```js
// #206796 lab proof: bridge OpenClaw's OpenAI-compatible request to a REAL local model (llama.cpp llama-server).
// The prompt is rendered by llama.cpp's own chat template from OpenClaw's exact messages + tool declarations
// (/apply-template), then decoded with NO tool-name grammar (llama.cpp's tool grammar only admits declared names,
// which is the very call under test). The model's <tool_call> output is parsed back into OpenAI tool_calls. Loopback only.
import http from "node:http";
import fs from "node:fs";
const [port, logFile, upstream = "http://127.0.0.1:8080"] = process.argv.slice(2);
const post = async (path, body) => { const r = await fetch(upstream + path, { method: "POST", headers: { "content-type": "application/json" }, body: JSON.stringify(body) }); if (!r.ok) throw new Error(path + " " + r.status + " " + (await r.text()).slice(0, 300)); return r.json(); };
// Streamed so a long prompt-processing phase never hits fetch's 300 s headers timeout.
async function complete(body) {
  const r = await fetch(upstream + "/completion", { method: "POST", headers: { "content-type": "application/json" }, body: JSON.stringify(body) });
  if (!r.ok) throw new Error("/completion " + r.status + " " + (await r.text()).slice(0, 300));
  let buf = "", out = "";
  const dec = new TextDecoder();
  for await (const part of r.body) {
    buf += dec.decode(part, { stream: true });
    let i;
    while ((i = buf.indexOf("\n")) >= 0) {
      const line = buf.slice(0, i).trim(); buf = buf.slice(i + 1);
      if (line.startsWith("data: ")) { try { out += JSON.parse(line.slice(6)).content ?? ""; } catch {} }
    }
  }
  return out;
}
function parseCalls(text) {
  const calls = [];
  for (const m of text.matchAll(/<tool_call>([\s\S]*?)(?:<\/tool_call>|$)/g)) {
    const b = m[1].trim();
    const fn = b.match(/<function=([^>\s]+)>([\s\S]*?)(?:<\/function>|$)/);
    if (fn) {
      const args = {};
      for (const p of fn[2].matchAll(/<parameter=([^>\s]+)>\n?([\s\S]*?)\n?<\/parameter>/g)) { let v = p[2]; try { const j = JSON.parse(v); if (j !== null && typeof j === "object") v = j; } catch {} args[p[1]] = v; }
      calls.push({ name: fn[1], args });
    } else { try { const j = JSON.parse(b); calls.push({ name: j.name, args: j.arguments ?? j.parameters ?? {} }); } catch {} }
  }
  return calls.map((c, i) => ({ id: "call_real_" + Date.now() + "_" + i, type: "function", function: { name: c.name, arguments: typeof c.args === "string" ? c.args : JSON.stringify(c.args) } }));
}
http.createServer((req, res) => {
  let raw = "";
  req.on("data", (c) => (raw += c));
  req.on("end", async () => {
    if (!req.url.includes("/chat/completions")) { res.writeHead(200, { "content-type": "application/json" }); res.end(JSON.stringify({ data: [{ id: "bonsai2-27b", object: "model" }] })); return; }
    const body = JSON.parse(raw || "{}");
    fs.appendFileSync(logFile + ".raw", raw + "\n");
    const tools = (body.tools || []).map((t) => t.function?.name ?? t.name);
    const stream = body.stream === true;
    let ka;
    if (stream) { res.writeHead(200, { "content-type": "text/event-stream", "cache-control": "no-cache" }); ka = setInterval(() => res.write(": keepalive\n\n"), 5000); }
    const t0 = Date.now();
    let out = "", calls = [], content = "", err;
    try {
      const { prompt } = await post("/apply-template", { messages: body.messages, tools: body.tools });
      out = await complete({ prompt, n_predict: 2048, temperature: 0.6, top_p: 0.95, cache_prompt: true, stream: true });
      const visible = out.includes("</think>") ? out.slice(out.lastIndexOf("</think>") + 8) : out;
      calls = parseCalls(visible);
      content = visible.replace(/<tool_call>[\s\S]*?(<\/tool_call>|$)/g, "").trim();
    } catch (e) { err = String(e); content = "bridge error: " + err; }
    clearInterval(ka);
    fs.appendFileSync(logFile, JSON.stringify({ at: new Date().toISOString(), ms: Date.now() - t0, declaredTools: tools, roles: (body.messages || []).map((m) => m.role), modelOutput: out.slice(-1500), toolCalls: calls.map((c) => c.function), err }) + "\n");
    const id = "chatcmpl-real", created = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000), model = body.model, finish = calls.length ? "tool_calls" : "stop";
    const usage = { prompt_tokens: 0, completion_tokens: 0, total_tokens: 0 };
    if (!stream) { res.writeHead(200, { "content-type": "application/json" }); res.end(JSON.stringify({ id, object: "chat.completion", created, model, choices: [{ index: 0, message: { role: "assistant", content: content || null, tool_calls: calls.length ? calls : undefined }, finish_reason: finish }], usage })); return; }
    const chunk = (delta, fr, extra = {}) => res.write("data: " + JSON.stringify({ id, object: "chat.completion.chunk", created, model, choices: [{ index: 0, delta, finish_reason: fr }], ...extra }) + "\n\n");
    if (content) chunk({ role: "assistant", content }, null);
    if (calls.length) chunk({ role: "assistant", tool_calls: calls.map((t, index) => ({ index, ...t })) }, null);
    chunk({}, finish, { usage });
    res.write("data: [DONE]\n\n"); res.end();
  });
}).listen(Number(port), "127.0.0.1", () => console.log("real-model bridge listening " + port));
